      {
        "heading": "1 INTRODUCETION",
        "text": "Offline reinforcement learning (RL) offers a promising framework to learn performant sequential policies in a supervised manner, which is highly preferred in many real-world problems where active data collection and exploration is expensive, unsafe, or infeasible (Swaminathan & Joachims, 2015; Shalev-Shwartz et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2020). However, this approach can still be expensive since labeling large datasets with rewards can be costly and requires huge human efforts. On the contrary, unlabeled data can be cheap and abundant, encouraging us to learn from self-supervisions with unlabeled data. Such self-supervised methods have achieved great success in computer vision (CV) and natural language processing (NLP) tasks like Brown et al. (2020); Devlin et al. (2018); Chen et al. (2020), but it is less discussed in the context of offline reinforcement learning.\nSome prior works (Ho & Ermon, 2016; Reddy et al., 2019; Kostrikov et al., 2019) have explored demonstration-based approaches, which remove the need for reward annotations but require the samples to be near optimal. There is also a line of work that focuses on data sharing from different datasets (Kalashnikov et al., 2021; Yu et al., 2021a) but assumes that the dataset can be relabeled with oracle reward functions for the target task. These assumptions can be strong in real-world problems, where both expert trajectories and reward labeling are expensive. It is important but non-trivial to incorporate unlabeled datasets into offline RL due to RL problems\u2019 sequential and dynamic nature. Yu et al. (2022) show that naively using a learned reward function to annotate the unsupervised dataset can lead to suboptimal performance, which is even worse than labeling the unsupervised dataset with all zeros. This naturally leads to the following question:\nHow can we leverage unlabeled data to improve the performance of offline RL algorithms in a principled way?\nTo answer this question, we start from the analysis of the theoretical benefit of using unlabeled data in linear MDPs. Intuitively, unlabeled data helps us gain more information about the dynamics of the MDP, leaving only uncertainty over reward functions. As a result, the problem is reduced to some sort of linear bandits when unlabeled data goes to infinity, which improves the performance bound accordingly. Based on this analysis, we may develop a model-based approach and add pessimism over models and rewards, respectively, but it can be computationally extensive. To sidestep this issue, we propose a model-free method, which we refer to as provable data sharing (PDS), which\nsimply adds uncertainty penalties on learned rewards to keep the algorithm conservative. We show that our method can cooperate with model-free offline methods while being simple and provably efficient. Theoretical analysis shows that our method can improve performance bounds under mild conditions. In practice, our method also achieves superior performance. We conduct extensive experiments on various environments, including single-task domains like MuJoCo (Todorov et al., 2012) and kitchen, as well as multi-task domains like AntMaze and Meta-World (Yu et al., 2020a). Results show that our algorithm improves significantly over previous methods like UDS (Yu et al., 2022) and naive reward prediction methods.\nOur main contribution is the provable data sharing (PDS) algorithm, a novel method to utilize unsupervised data for offline RL with theoretical guarantees. PDS is a model-free method that adds uncertainty penalties upon learned reward functions and can be easily integrated with existing offline RL algorithms. We show that PDS can achieve provable improvement in linear MDPs under mild conditions. We also show that our method achieves superior performance in practice with extensive experiments on various tasks like locomotion, navigation, and manipulation tasks."
