      {
        "heading": "5 EXPERIMENTS",
        "text": "In this section, we aim to evaluate the effectiveness of pessimistic reward estimation and answer the following questions: (1) How does PDS perform compared to the naive reward prediction and unlabeled data sharing (UDS) methods in single locomotion and manipulation tasks? (2) How does PDS behave in multi-task offline RL settings? (3) Why and when is PDS effective?\nSingle-task domains and datasets. To answer Question (1), we perform empirical evaluations on the hopper, walker2d, and kitchen tasks adopted from D4RL (Fu et al., 2020). In the first two tasks, we consider 50 labeled trajectories and various amounts of unlabeled trajectories of different sizes and qualities. On the kitchen task, we use the complete dataset as labeled data and use mixed\\partial datasets as unlabeled data. These two setups resemble practical problems where the labeled data is scarce.\nMulti-task domains and datasets. As for Question (2), we consider several multi-task domains. (1) The first set of domains is the Meta-World (Yu et al., 2020a). We consider the same setup as in CDS (Yu et al., 2021a) and four tasks: door open, door close, drawer open and drawer close. Each task has a sparse reward of 1 when the success condition is met and 0 otherwise. (2) The second domain is the AntMaze task in D4RL, which consists of mazes of two sizes (medium and large) with 3 and 7 tasks corresponding to different goal positions, respectively. Following Yu et al. (2021a; 2022), we construct two set of tasks, undirected and directed. The trajectories in the undirected dataset is randomly and uniformly divided into different tasks, while the directed dataset is associated with the goal closest to the final state of the trajectory. Please refer to Appendix D for detailed description.\nComparisons. We adopt the state-of-the-art unlabeled data sharing method (UDS) (Yu et al., 2022) as our baseline, and we combine UDS with IQL (Kostrikov et al., 2021), the same underlying offline RL method a