        "text": "Results of Question (1). We evaluate each method on the hopper, walker2d, and kitchen domains. As shown in Table 3, PDS outperforms other methods on most tasks and achieves competitive or better performance than the oracle method. Particularly, PDS performs well when the labeled and unlabeled datasets have different data qualities. We hypothesize that this is because PDS successfully captures the uncertainties induced by this distribution shift and keeps the algorithm pessimistic. The prediction method performs well when the unlabeled dataset has a good quality, and UDS performs well when the unlabeled data has low quality. PDS takes the better of both worlds and achieves a better performance.\nResults of Question (2). Multi-task settings exhibit more significant distributional shifts between labeled data and unlabeled data due to the different goals of the tasks. To evaluate the performance of PDS on multi-task settings, we evaluate each method on the AntMaze and the Meta-World domain, and the result is shown in Table 1 and Table 2. On Meta-World experiments, the performance of\nPDS is comparable to the oracle method and outperforms other methods. UDS performs relatively poorly on this dataset. We hypothesize that this is because the dataset quality is relatively high, and labeling the dataset with zeros induces a large reward bias. On the multi-task AntMaze domain, PDS outperforms both UDS and the naive prediction method. The performance gap between PDS and other baselines is larger on the diverse dataset. This aligns with our observation on single-task domains that PDS performs better when the distribution shift between datasets is larger. Please refer to Table 4 in Appendix E for the complete comparison.\nResults of Question (3). To investigate the effect of uncertainty weights, we conduct experiments on the hopper and walker2d tasks with various penalty weight k, shown in Figure 1. The results show that PDS interpolates between UDS and the reward prediction method. PDS degenerates to naive reward prediction if k is small and performs like UDS when k is relatively large. PDS offers a better trade-off to balance the conservation and generalization of reward estimators and thus achieves better performance. We plot the standard deviation of state values learned by IQL between different runs to investigate further why PDS improves over naive prediction methods. The result in Figure 2 shows that PDS reduces the variance from reward prediction and is close to the oracle method. This indicates that PDS successfully reduces the uncertainties from the variance of reward predictors while keeping the reward bias small."
