        "heading": "6 CONCLUSION",
        "text": "This paper investigates the theoretical benefit of using unlabeled data in offline reinforcement learning. We show that unlabeled data helps us gain more information about the dynamics of the MDP, reduce the problem to linear bandits, and improve the performance bound accordingly. Based on this analysis, we propose a new method, PDS, which adds uncertainty penalties on learned rewards to keep the algorithm conservative. We show that our method had provable guarantees in theory and achieves superior performance in practice. Integrating representation learning methods for offline unsupervised data sharing is an interesting future direction. It is also an interesting direction to extend our analysis into more general settings than linear MDPs like generalized linear MDPs (Wang et al., 2019) and low-rank MDPs (Ayoub et al., 2020; Jiang et al., 2017)."
