    "abstractText": "Self-supervised methods play a vital role in fueling the progress of deep learning using supervision from the data itself, obviating the need for expensive annotations. The same merit applies to offline reinforcement learning (RL), which conducts RL in a supervised manner, but it is unclear how to utilize such unlabeled data to improve offline RL in a principled way. In this paper, we examine the theoretical benefit of unlabeled data in the context of linear MDPs and propose a novel and Provable Data Sharing algorithm, which we refer to as PDS, to utilize such unlabeled data for offline RL. PDS utilizes additional penalties upon the reward function learned from labeled data to avoid potential overestimation of the reward. We show that such a penalty is crucial to keep the algorithm conservative, and PDS achieves a provable benefit from unlabeled data under mild conditions. We conduct extensive experiments on various offline RL tasks and show that PDS can significantly improve offline RL algorithms with unlabeled data. 1 INTRODUCETION Offline reinforcement learning (RL) offers a promising framework to learn performant sequential policies in a supervised manner, which is highly preferred in many real-world problems where active data collection and exploration is expensive, unsafe, or infeasible (Swaminathan & Joachims, 2015; Shalev-Shwartz et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2020). However, this approach can still be expensive since labeling large datasets with rewards can be costly and requires huge human efforts. On the contrary, unlabeled data can be cheap and abundant, encouraging us to learn from self-supervisions with unlabeled data. Such self-supervised methods have achieved great success in computer vision (CV) and natural language processing (NLP) tasks like Brown et al. (2020); Devlin et al. (2018); Chen et al. (2020), but it is less discussed in the context of offline reinforcement learning. Some prior works (Ho & Ermon, 2016; Reddy et al., 2019; Kostrikov et al., 2019) have explored demonstration-based approaches, which remove the need for reward annotations but require the samples to be near optimal. There is also a line of work that focuses on data sharing from different datasets (Kalashnikov et al., 2021; Yu et al., 2021a) but assumes that the dataset can be relabeled with oracle reward functions for the target task. These assumptions can be strong in real-world problems, where both expert trajectories and reward labeling are expensive. It is important but non-trivial to incorporate unlabeled datasets into offline RL due to RL problems\u2019 sequential and dynamic nature. Yu et al. (2022) show that naively using a learned reward function to annotate the unsupervised dataset can lead to suboptimal performance, which is even worse than labeling the unsupervised dataset with all zeros. This naturally leads to the following question: How can we leverage unlabeled data to improve the performance of offline RL algorithms in a principled way? To answer this question, we start from the analysis of the theoretical benefit of using unlabeled data in linear MDPs. Intuitively, unlabeled data helps us gain more information about the dynamics of the MDP, leaving only uncertainty over reward functions. As a result, the problem is reduced to some sort of linear bandits when unlabeled data goes to infinity, which improves the performance bound accordingly. Based on this analysis, we may develop a model-based approach and add pessimism over models and rewards, respectively, but it can be computationally extensive. To sidestep this issue, we propose a model-free method, which we refer to as provable data sharing (PDS), which",
